  "grammar_notes": [
    "Numbers for seven and eight appear to be derived from roots for two and three",
    "Contains modal suffix marking potential states",
    "Number words might represent subtractive formations (e.g., 'ten lacking two')",
    "Object-verb syntax",
    "Reduplication signals frequency or intensity",
    "Reduplication used with verb and noun roots",
    "Third person singular subjects were unmarked",
    "Pronominal objects marked by proclitics",
    "Third person singular objects unmarked",
    "Words show nasalization of r's to n's due to medial nasal vowel",
    "Some words contain independent modal suffix |-re|",
    "Some words can end with interrogative mode marker |-ne|",
    "Third person singular affix |-h-|",
    "Independent mode marker |-re|",
    "Second person singular prefix |ya-|"
  ],
  "pronunciation_notes": [
    "First 'n' in some words might be an inverted 'u'",
    "Four short oral vowels: i, e, a, u",
    "Four long oral vowels: i-, e-, a-, u-",
    "Four nasal vowels: į, ę, ą, ų",
    "Most Woccon words show a long oral vowel"
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    "Data sourced from John Lawson's 1860 text 'A new voyage to Carolina'",
    "Appears to be a historical documentation of Woccon language by an early colonial explorer",
    "Waccon towns included Yupwauremau and Tooptatmeer",
    "Woccon language documented by Adelung and Vater in 1806",
    "Language studied by Richard T. Carter in 1980",
    "Original documentation by Lawson in 1709 contains potential transcription errors",
    "Linguistic source: International Journal of American Linguistics",
    "Woccon is a Siouan language",
    "Closely related to Catawba language",
    "Documented by Lawson and scholars like Rudes",
    "Phonology reconstructed using Catawba cognates",
    "Reduplication involves repeating entire root",
    "Roo or Ru might mean 'thing or nothing'",
    "Woccon is a Siouan language related to Catawba",
    "Comparative linguistic analysis shows word relationships between Woccon and Catawba languages",
    "Waccamaw name possibly derived from 'waką́n ma' meaning 'place of power, sacred place'",
    "Postposition 'mau' in Woccon similar to Catawba postposition 'ma'",
    "Woccon is a Siouan language from North Carolina",
    "Historical documentation includes sources from 1709 (Lawson) to 2024 (Passmore et al.)",
    "Multiple linguistic sources have studied Woccon language, including Adelung, Vater, Carter, and Campbell",
    "Language documentation shows variation in spelling and transcription over time",
    "Woccon is an Eastern Siouan language",
    "Woccon was most closely related to Catawba within the Siouan linguistic family",
    "Woccon is considered a dormant language",
    "Woccon was a subdivision of the Waccamaw",
    "Waccamaw territory extended from Winyah Bay up Waccamaw River to the Cape Fear, and somewhat to the Neuse River",
    "Woccon was one of two Catawban languages in the Siouan language family"
  ]
